  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 264.72pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 291.36pt, "Multilayered thin-film structures are frequently encountered in industrial applications, where accurate thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 316.56pt, "property characterization is essential for performance optimization. These films, typically ranging from ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 342pt, "nanometers to micrometers in thickness, often exhibit anisotropic thermal conductivity and non-bulk heat ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 367.2pt, "capacity, which are challenging to measure. In this study, we introduce a variable-spot-size and multi-")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 392.4pt, "frequency square-pulsed source (SPS) method for the simultaneous determination of anisotropic thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 417.84pt, "conductivities, heat capacities, and interfacial thermal conductance in multilayered systems. By leveraging a ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 443.04pt, "broad modulation frequency range (1 Hz to 10 MHz) and tunable laser spot sizes, the SPS method enhances ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 468.48pt, "sensitivity to different thermal parameters across layers. We validate this approach on a silicon-on-insulator ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 493.68pt, "(SOI) sample comprising a 1.59 µm Si layer, 1.03 µm SiO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(328.529pt, 494.64pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(332.028pt, 493.68pt, " layer, and a silicon substrate with a 122 nm ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 518.88pt, "aluminum (Al) transducer. The SPS method successfully extracts seven key thermal parameters, including the ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 544.32pt, "in-plane and cross-plane thermal conductivities and heat capacity of the Si film, the thermal conductivity and ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 569.52pt, "heat capacity of the SiO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(174.164pt, 570.48pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(177.664pt, 569.52pt, " layer, the thermal conductivity of the substrate, and the interfacial thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 594.96pt, "conductance between Al and Si. Temperature-dependent measurements from 80 to 500 K showed excellent ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 620.16pt, "agreement with literature values and first-principles predictions, confirming the method’s accuracy and ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 645.36pt, "reliability. These results demonstrate the SPS method as a powerful tool for comprehensive thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 670.8pt, "characterization of complex multilayered structures, with implications for both fundamental research and ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 696pt, "practical applications.   ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 722.4pt, "Keywords: ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(112.16pt, 722.4pt, "square-pulsed source (SPS) method, anisotropic thermal conductivity, heat capacity, multilayered ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 749.04pt, "thin films, pump-probe technique")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(595pt, 842pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.705pt, 804.24pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 68.16pt, "1. Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 94.8pt, "Thin-film structures, particularly those in the nanometer to micrometer range, play a critical role in modern ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 120pt, "industrial applications, ranging from microelectronics [1, 2] and optoelectronics [3] to thermal management in ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 145.44pt, "advanced devices [4]. These films often exhibit complex thermal behavior, such as anisotropic thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 170.64pt, "conductivity [5] and deviations in volumetric heat capacity from bulk values [6]. Accurate measurement of ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 196.08pt, "these thermal properties is essential for device performance and reliability. However, conventional techniques ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 221.28pt, "such as calorimetry and steady-state methods are often inadequate for thin films, especially in multilayered ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 246.72pt, "systems where heat transfer is strongly influenced by interfacial effects and anisotropy. ")
  #line-at(73.38pt, 271.92pt, "Among existing approaches, pump-probe techniques such as time-domain thermoreflectance (TDTR) [7, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 297.12pt, "8] and frequency-domain thermoreflectance (FDTR) [9] have become popular for characterizing thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 322.56pt, "properties at the micro- and nanoscale. These methods use a modulated pump laser to heat the sample and a ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 347.76pt, "probe laser to monitor temperature-induced reflectance changes. While powerful, their limited frequency range ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 373.2pt, "and sensitivity trade-offs often prevent comprehensive extraction of thermal conductivities (both in-plane and ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 398.4pt, "cross-plane), heat capacities, and interfacial thermal transport properties in layered film systems within a single ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 423.6pt, "framework.  ")
  #line-at(73.38pt, 449.04pt, "To overcome these challenges, we introduce the square-pulsed source (SPS) method―a versatile pump-")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 474.24pt, "probe technique capable of comprehensive thermal characterization [10-12]. By using a square-wave ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 499.68pt, "modulated pump beam and analyzing time-resolved reflectance signals over a broad frequency range (1 Hz to ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 524.88pt, "10 MHz) and varied laser spot sizes, the SPS method enhances sensitivity to both in-plane and cross-plane ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 550.08pt, "transport across multiple layers.  ")
  #line-at(73.38pt, 575.52pt, "In this work, we apply the SPS method to a silicon-on-insulator (SOI) sample to demonstrate its ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 600.72pt, "effectiveness. The method enables the simultaneous extraction of seven key thermal parameters, including the ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 626.16pt, "in-plane and cross-plane thermal conductivities and heat capacity of the Si film, thermal conductivity and heat ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 651.36pt, "capacity of the SiO₂ layer, thermal conductivity of the substrate, and interfacial thermal conductance between ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 676.56pt, "Al and Si. Results over 80-500 K show excellent agreement with literature and theoretical predictions, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 702pt, "validating the SPS method as an accurate and versatile tool for multilayer thin-film thermal characterization. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 753.6pt, "2. Methodologies ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 780.24pt, "2.1. Basic principle of the SPS technique ")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(595pt, 842pt, (image("graphics/page-03.png", width: 476pt), 58pt, 462pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.705pt, 804.24pt, "3 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 67.2pt, "The SPS technique, illustrated schematically in Fig. 1(a), employs a pump laser modulated with a 50% duty ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 92.4pt, "cycle square wave to generate periodic heating on the sample surface. A probe laser, focused on the same spot, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 117.84pt, "detects the resulting temperature variations through the thermoreflectance effect. The reflected probe laser ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 143.04pt, "beam is captured by a photodetector, which converts the optical signals into corresponding electrical signals. ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 168.48pt, "These signals are processed by a periodic waveform analyzer, an integrated advanced function of the Zurich ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 193.68pt, "UHF lock-in amplifier, to produce time-resolved voltage signals over a complete square-wave heating cycle ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 219.12pt, "with a high signal-to-noise ratio. The resulting voltage signals, directly proportional to the sample’s ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 244.32pt, "temperature response, undergo normalization along both amplitude and time axes. Finally, a thermal model is ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 269.52pt, "applied to best fit the normalized data, enabling accurate determination of the specimen’s thermal properties ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 294.96pt, "[10, 13].  ")
  #line-at(78.88pt, 320.16pt, "In our system, the pump and probe lasers operate at 458 nm and 785 nm, respectively. To conduct the ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 345.6pt, "measurements, the sample is typically coated with a ~100 nm thick aluminum (Al) film as a transducer layer. ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 370.8pt, "With the Al film thickness being significantly greater than its optical skin depth (~6.2 nm at 458 nm), all ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 396pt, "incident optical energy is absorbed near the surface. Therefore, heat generation is confined within the Al film, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 421.44pt, "and thermal transport into the underlying layers occurs solely through diffusion. This assumption underpins ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 446.64pt, "the surface heating boundary condition used in our thermal model fitting. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 672.48pt, "Figure 1.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(107.378pt, 672.48pt, "(a) Schematic diagram of the experimental setup for the SPS method. (b) Schematic illustrating ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 691.44pt, "individual and combined thermal parameters in an ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(286.986pt, 691.44pt, "N")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.324pt, 691.44pt, "-layered thin-film structure. The figure highlights how ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 710.4pt, "different parameters contribute to the system’s thermal response. These parameters form the basis for ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 729.36pt, "sensitivity analysis and signal fitting in SPS measurements.  ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 773.76pt, "2.2. Principle of measuring multiple parameters in multilayered systems ")
]

